#{[17592186320904 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320929 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }5{x}^{3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186320986 :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321011 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$"] [17592186321070 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{(1+\\frac{4}{n})}^{2}}{{(1+\\frac{5}{n})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186321073 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186321075 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+4}{n+5}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\n$$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186323682 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [17592186323718 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323719 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+11}{12{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{\\left(\\frac{12{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{x}^{5}})}{(12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+0+0}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [17592186323720 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"] [17592186323721 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}-9x+1}{x+4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}-\\frac{9x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x-9+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(4x)-9 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186323722 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{7}+1}{10{x}^{3}+x+3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{5{x}^{7}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-5{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(10+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{4}+0}{10+0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186323723 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323732 :question/worked-solution "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186323752 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323753 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"] [17592186323800 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}+4(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4x({n}^{2}+4n)}{{n}^{2}+6n+5}| \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}+4n}{{n}^{2}+6n+5} \\\\  & =|4x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|4x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|4x|<1,$ which means $|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{4},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{4},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the direct comparison test with the convergent $p$-series $\\sum \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}.$ So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{x}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}{(-1/4)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+4n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5}+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7}+\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 8}-⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186323844 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{-x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186323844 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{-x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\sqrt{-x}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{-0}+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186323845 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$"] [17592186323845 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}x \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323846 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}⁡{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$"] [17592186323847 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3\\cdot 3-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186323859 :question/problem "Find the left-sided limit at $x=4$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{x} & \\text{ for }\\,0\\le x<4 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ for }\\,x=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2} & \\text{ for }\\,x>4.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186323859 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323899 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+3{cot}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2}+3{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =3+3\\left(\\frac{3}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323924 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323933 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-1]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =4(1)^{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323934 :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[5{x}^{2}-3x-14]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{10x-3}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(2)-3}{2(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323941 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}}{[x+16]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323942 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2{x}^{3}-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186327874 :answer/value "q(x)=\\begin{cases}x-3, & x<3 \\\\ x+3, & x\\ge 3\\end{cases}"] [17592186329758 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+5}-\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{x+5}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{{(\\sqrt{x+5})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x+5-5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5})}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+5}+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329759 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}{{3}^{2}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{{x}^{2}-9} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}{(x-3)(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x+1-4}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329761 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{1-1}{1-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-\\sqrt{x})(x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{1+\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329795 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329796 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329797 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\frac{64-8\\sqrt{64}}{64-64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x-8\\sqrt{x}}{x-64} & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{(x-8\\sqrt{x})(x+8\\sqrt{x})}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{(8\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64x}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x(x-64)}{(x-64)(x+8\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (64)^{-}}\\frac{x}{x+8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64+8\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329798 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & =\\frac{50-10\\sqrt{25}}{25-25}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x-10\\sqrt{x}}{x-25} & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})}{x-25} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2(x-5\\sqrt{x})(x+5\\sqrt{x})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{(5\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2({x}^{2}-25x)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x(x-25)}{(x-25)(x+5\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {25}^{-}}\\frac{2x}{x+5\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(25)}{25+5\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329802 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9d0e9d944cda9c97555ffdeac5aa245905f800cd99e11f5d172203bad3892d.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi $ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186329826 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true."] [17592186329840 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186329840 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[0.5,1]$ only."] [17592186329853 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$” is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists” is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186329854 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186329855 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$” is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"] [17592186329856 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"] [17592186329862 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-{x}^{2}, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ -2x, & x>2\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186329862 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=4,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-2x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-4,$ while $f(2)=4.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2).$"] [17592186329863 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -x, & x>1\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186329863 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$"] [17592186329865 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}x+4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1, & x>1,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{x+3}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329865 :question/worked-solution "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1+4=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(x+4)=1+4=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}+1)=3(1)^{2}+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(1)=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+3})=\\sqrt{1+3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=2=g(1).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}h(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\ne h(1).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$"] [17592186329866 :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{cases} \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329866 :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$"] [17592186329867 :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}\\operatorname{sin}(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{cases}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329867 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$"] [17592186329869 :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & =\\begin{cases}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{cases} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & =\\begin{cases}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{cases}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329869 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$"] [17592186329873 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186329875 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{2}$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}}-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329876 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}+5) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329878 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\begin{cases}3x-6,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186329878 :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$”; “$g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$”."] [17592186329912 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|7x-4|=7x-4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7-\\frac{4}{x})}{(2-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329915 :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|3x+6|=-(3x+6).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(3+0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$"] [17592186329929 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|6x-2|=6x-2.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{8x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6-\\frac{2}{x})}{(8+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329932 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}}{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329933 :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|10x+3|=10x+3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}=\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{10x+3}{6-5x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{10x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{5x}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10+\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329940 :question/worked-solution "For sufficiently large negative values of $x,$ we have $|6x-4|=-(6x-4)=-6x+4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-6+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332671 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332672 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=5{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332780 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332781 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332782 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration. \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}6{x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7{x}^{6/7}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}7\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{6}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{1}^{6}}-7\\cdot \\sqrt[7]{{a}^{6}}] \\\\  & =7(1)-7(0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332823 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 3, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186332838 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|2x| \\\\  & =2|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186332840 :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges."] [17592186332842 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+1-1}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332845 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false and statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$"] [17592186332946 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332961 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡z]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6) \\\\  & =\\infty -\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [17592186332975 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335802 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186335841 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335934 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335942 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-1)^{3}-2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 10.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186335943 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335944 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335945 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(4{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{2}-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335946 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186335947 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186335948 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186335951 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335957 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335958 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335994 :question/worked-solution "To find the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $4$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  & 1 &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 &  &  &  \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 &  &  \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 &  \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})=6.$"] [17592186335996 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}\\right) &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}\\right) &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}\\right) &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}\\right) &  \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right).$"] [17592186335998 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $5$ and position $4$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccccccc} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}\\right) &  &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2}\\right) &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3}\\right) &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}\\right) &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}\\right) &  & \\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5}\\right) &  \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\left(\\frac{4}{4}\\right).$"]}
